𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟭𝟰/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗦𝘂𝗱𝗱𝗲𝗻𝗹𝘆 𝗦𝗹𝗼𝘄 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your application was working normally, but suddenly response time increased. The pods are still Running. What will you check?”

I wouldn't immediately restart the pods.

First, I would identify where the latency is coming from.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check pod resource usage

kubectl top pods -n <namespace>

Look for unusual CPU or memory consumption.

2️⃣ Check pod health

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Verify:

READY status

Restarts

Pod age

Number of replicas


3️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for:

🔹 Slow requests
🔹 Timeouts
🔹 Exceptions
🔹 Database connection issues
🔹 External API failures

4️⃣ Check Kubernetes events

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for resource pressure, probe failures, or other recent events.

5️⃣ Check dependencies

If CPU and memory look normal, I would investigate:

👉 Database latency
👉 External APIs
👉 Cache
👉 Network connectivity
👉 Service-to-service communication

6️⃣ Check recent changes

I would also check for:

🔹 Recent deployment
🔹 Configuration changes
🔹 Traffic increase
🔹 Database changes
🔹 Infrastructure changes

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

User → Ingress/LB → Service → Pod → Application → Database/External Services

The goal is not just to see that the pod is Running.

The goal is to find where the latency starts.

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Running ≠ Performing Well.

A healthy-looking Kubernetes status doesn't always mean the application is healthy from the user's perspective.
